<section xml:id="cmse381-lec06-multlinreg-worksheet" component="ws06">
  <title>Day 06 Worksheet</title>
  <introduction>
    <p>In the last few lectures, we have focused on single input linear regression, that is, fitting models of the form</p>
    <p><md>
    Y =  \beta_0 +  \beta_1 X + \varepsilon
    </md></p>
    <p>In this lab, we will continue to use two different tools for linear regression.</p>
    <p><ul>
      <li>
        <p><url href="https://scikit-learn.org/stable/index.html">Scikit learn</url> is arguably the most used tool for machine learning in python</p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p><url href="https://www.statsmodels.org">Statsmodels</url> provides many of the statisitcial tests we've been learning in class</p>
      </li>
    </ul></p>
    <program language="python">
      # As always, we start with our favorite standard imports. 

      import numpy as np
      import pandas as pd
      import matplotlib.pyplot as plt 
      %matplotlib inline
      import seaborn as sns
      import statsmodels.formula.api as smf
      from sklearn.linear_model import LinearRegression
    </program>
  </introduction>
  <subsection xml:id="cmse381-lec06-multlinreg-worksheet-multiple-linear-regression">
    <title>Multiple linear regression</title>
    <p>Next we get some code up and running that can do linear regression with multiple input variables, that is when the model is of the form</p>
    <p><md>
    Y =  \beta_0 +  \beta_1 X_1 +  \beta_2 X_2 + \cdots +  \beta_pX_p + \varepsilon
    </md></p>
    <program language="python">
      from sklearn.datasets import load_diabetes
      diabetes = load_diabetes(as_frame=True)
      diabetes_df = pd.DataFrame(diabetes.data, columns = diabetes.feature_names)
      diabetes_df['target'] = pd.Series(diabetes.target)

      diabetes_df
    </program>
    <p>We first model <c>target = beta_0 + beta_1 *s1 + beta_2 * s5</c> using <c>scikitlearn</c>.</p>
    <program language="python">
      X = diabetes_df[['s1','s5']].values
      y = diabetes_df['target'].values

      multireg = LinearRegression() #&lt;----- notice I'm using exactly the same command as above
      multireg.fit(X,y)

      print(multireg.coef_)
      print(multireg.intercept_)
    </program>
    <figure><caption>Stop Icon</caption><image source="https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1e/Vienna_Convention_road_sign_B2a.svg/180px-Vienna_Convention_road_sign_B2a.svg.png"><description><p>Stop Icon</p></description></image></figure>
    <p>Great, you got to here! Hang out for a bit, there's more lecture before we go on to the next portion.</p>
    <program language="python">
      # We're going to use the (fake) data set used in the beginning of the book. 
      url = "https://msu-cmse-courses.github.io/CMSE381-S26/_downloads/27085b33898cca7fb57dc6897c2deccd/Advertising.csv"
      advertising_df = pd.read_csv(url, index_col = 0)
      advertising_df
    </program>
    <figure><caption>Stop Icon</caption><image source="https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1e/Vienna_Convention_road_sign_B2a.svg/180px-Vienna_Convention_road_sign_B2a.svg.png"><description><p>Stop Icon</p></description></image></figure>
    <p>Great, you got to here! Hang out for a bit, there's more lecture before we go on to the next portion.</p>
    <!-- horizontal rule in source notebook -->
    <exercises>
      <title>Exercises</title>
      <exercisegroup>
        <title>Practice</title>
        <introduction>
          <p>Complete the following exercises.</p>
        </introduction>
        <exercise>
          <task>
            <statement>
              <p>What are the values for <m>\beta_0</m>, <m>\beta_1</m>, and <m>\beta_2</m>?</p>
            </statement>
            <hint>
              <p>In scikit-learn, <c>intercept_</c> is <m>\beta_0</m>, while <c>coef_</c> contains the slope coefficients.</p>
            </hint>
            <hint>
              <p>Match the entries of <c>coef_</c> to the column order used to create <c>X</c>: first <c>s1</c>, then <c>s5</c>.</p>
            </hint>
            <hint>
              <p><alert>Common error:</alert> Do not treat the first entry of <c>coef_</c> as the intercept; the intercept is printed separately.</p>
            </hint>
            <answer>
              <p><m>\beta_0=152.1335</m>, <m>\beta_1=-175.7111</m> for <c>s1</c>, and <m>\beta_2=1006.7170</m> for <c>s5</c>.</p>
            </answer>
          </task>
        </exercise>
        <exercise>
          <task>
            <statement>
              <p>Write an interpretation for the <m>\beta_2</m> value in this data set.</p>
            </statement>
            <hint>
              <p>Start with: "For a one-unit increase in <c>s5</c>, the predicted <c>target</c> changes by ..."</p>
            </hint>
            <hint>
              <p>Because this is a multiple-regression coefficient, include the phrase "holding <c>s1</c> constant."</p>
            </hint>
            <hint>
              <p><alert>Common error:</alert> A fitted regression coefficient describes an association; by itself it does not establish that changing <c>s5</c> causes <c>target</c> to change.</p>
            </hint>
            <answer>
              <p>Holding <c>s1</c> constant, a one-unit increase in <c>s5</c> is associated with an expected increase of about <m>1006.72</m> units in <c>target</c>.</p>
            </answer>
          </task>
        </exercise>
        <exercise>
          <task>
            <statement>
              <p>We next model <c>target = beta_0 + beta_1 *s1 + beta_2 * s5</c> using <c>statsmodels</c>. Do you get the same model?</p>
              <program language="python">
                <code>
                  # Fit the same model with statsmodels
                  multiple_est = smf.ols('target ~ s1 + s5', data=diabetes_df).fit()
                  multiple_est.summary()
                </code>
              </program>
            </statement>
            <hint>
              <p>Compare the <c>Intercept</c>, <c>s1</c>, and <c>s5</c> entries in the statsmodels coefficient table with the scikit-learn output.</p>
            </hint>
            <hint>
              <p>Small differences in the last displayed decimal are only rounding differences.</p>
            </hint>
            <hint>
              <p><alert>Common error:</alert> Compare the fitted coefficients when deciding whether the models match; the additional inferential statistics do not make it a different fitted model.</p>
            </hint>
            <answer>
              <p>Yes. Statsmodels estimates <c>Intercept = 152.1335</c>, <c>s1 = -175.7111</c>, and <c>s5 = 1006.7170</c>, matching the scikit-learn model up to rounding.</p>
            </answer>
          </task>
        </exercise>
        <exercise>
          <task>
            <statement>
              <p>What is the predicted model?</p>
            </statement>
            <hint>
              <p>Write the fitted-value equation as <m>\widehat{y}=\widehat{\beta}_0+\widehat{\beta}_1s1+\widehat{\beta}_2s5</m>.</p>
            </hint>
            <hint>
              <p>Substitute the intercept and the two slopes, keeping the negative sign on the <c>s1</c> coefficient.</p>
            </hint>
            <hint>
              <p><alert>Common error:</alert> Do not omit the intercept or turn the negative <c>s1</c> coefficient into a positive term.</p>
            </hint>
            <answer>
              <p><m>\widehat{y}=152.1335-175.7111\,s1+1006.7170\,s5</m>.</p>
            </answer>
          </task>
        </exercise>
        <exercise>
          <task>
            <statement>
              <p>How much trust can we place in the estimates?</p>
            </statement>
            <hint>
              <p>Use the <c>P&gt;|t|</c> column and the 95% confidence intervals to assess whether each slope is distinguishable from zero.</p>
            </hint>
            <hint>
              <p>Then separate evidence about the coefficients from broader questions about model assumptions, predictive performance, and causality.</p>
            </hint>
            <hint>
              <p><alert>Common error:</alert> Small p-values do not prove that the model is correct, that its assumptions hold, or that the relationships are causal.</p>
            </hint>
            <answer>
              <p>At the <m>\alpha=0.05</m> level, both slopes are statistically distinguishable from zero (<c>s1</c>: <m>p=0.018</m>; <c>s5</c>: <m>p&lt;0.001</m>). The <c>s1</c> interval is relatively wide, however, and these results are conditional on the linear-model assumptions. They support association, not causation.</p>
            </answer>
          </task>
        </exercise>
        <exercise>
          <task>
            <statement>
              <p>Run the linear regression to predict <c>target</c> using all the other variables. What do you notice about the different terms? Are some more related than others?</p>
              <program language="python">
                <code>
                  # Build a list containing every predictor column.
                  cols = ...

                  # Fit target using all predictors with statsmodels.
                  model = ...
                  multiple_est = ...
                  multiple_est.summary()
                </code>
              </program>
            </statement>
            <hint>
              <p>The predictor columns are every column except <c>target</c>; you can form the statsmodels formula with <c>' + '.join(cols)</c>.</p>
            </hint>
            <hint>
              <p>In the fitted summary, compare the coefficient signs and magnitudes, then use <c>P&gt;|t|</c> to assess each variable's additional contribution after controlling for the others.</p>
            </hint>
            <hint>
              <p><alert>Common error:</alert> A large coefficient is not automatically the strongest relationship, and a nonsignificant partial coefficient does not prove that a variable has no relationship with <c>target</c>.</p>
            </hint>
            <answer>
              <p>The estimated coefficients differ substantially. At <m>\alpha=0.05</m>, <c>sex</c>, <c>bmi</c>, <c>bp</c>, and <c>s5</c> have statistically significant partial associations with <c>target</c>; <c>s1</c> is close but has <m>p=0.058</m>, and <c>age</c>, <c>s2</c>, <c>s3</c>, <c>s4</c>, and <c>s6</c> are not significant after the other variables are included.</p>
              <program language="python">
                <code>
                  cols = [column for column in diabetes_df.columns if column != 'target']
                  model = 'target ~ ' + ' + '.join(cols)
                  multiple_est = smf.ols(model, data=diabetes_df).fit()
                  multiple_est.summary()
                </code>
              </program>
            </answer>
          </task>
        </exercise>
        <exercise>
          <task>
            <statement>
              <p>Using the statsmodels summary from the previous exercise, which predictors have statistically significant partial associations with <c>target</c> at <m>\alpha=0.05</m>? What does "partial association" mean here?</p>
            </statement>
            <hint>
              <p>Compare each predictor's <c>P&gt;|t|</c> value with <m>0.05</m>.</p>
            </hint>
            <hint>
              <p>Each slope is tested while the remaining predictors in the model are held fixed.</p>
            </hint>
            <hint>
              <p><alert>Common error:</alert> Do not say that a nonsignificant coefficient proves there is no relationship; it means the model did not detect a unique linear contribution after adjustment for the other predictors.</p>
            </hint>
            <answer>
              <p><c>sex</c>, <c>bmi</c>, <c>bp</c>, and <c>s5</c> have <m>p&lt;0.05</m>. Here, a partial association is the estimated relationship between one predictor and <c>target</c> after controlling for all other predictors in the fitted model.</p>
            </answer>
          </task>
        </exercise>
        <exercise>
          <task>
            <statement>
              <p>Earlier you determined the p-value for the <c>s1</c> variable when we only used <c>s1</c> to predict <c>target</c>. What changed about the p-value for <c>s1</c> now where it is part of a regression using all the variables. Why?</p>
              <program language="python">
                <code>
                  # Compare the one-predictor and all-predictor models.
                  just_s1 = smf.ols('target ~ s1', data=diabetes_df).fit()
                  print(just_s1.pvalues['s1'])
                  print(multiple_est.pvalues['s1'])

                  # Investigate correlation among predictors.
                  diabetes_df.corr()
                </code>
              </program>
            </statement>
            <hint>
              <p>Fit <c>target ~ s1</c>, then compare its <c>s1</c> p-value with the <c>s1</c> row of the all-predictor model.</p>
            </hint>
            <hint>
              <p>Inspect the predictor correlation matrix, especially the relationship between <c>s1</c> and <c>s2</c>, to understand why the unique information attributed to <c>s1</c> changes.</p>
            </hint>
            <hint>
              <p><alert>Common error:</alert> An increased p-value does not mean the data changed; it reflects a different conditional question after correlated predictors are added.</p>
            </hint>
            <answer>
              <p>The <c>s1</c> p-value rises from about <m>6.92\times10^{-6}</m> in the one-predictor model to about <m>0.058</m> in the all-predictor model. <c>s1</c> and <c>s2</c> are highly correlated (about <m>0.897</m>), so the full model has difficulty separating their unique contributions; this inflates uncertainty for the partial <c>s1</c> effect.</p>
              <program language="python">
                <code>
                  just_s1 = smf.ols('target ~ s1', data=diabetes_df).fit()
                  print(just_s1.pvalues['s1'])
                  print(multiple_est.pvalues['s1'])
                  print(diabetes_df[['s1', 's2']].corr())
                </code>
              </program>
            </answer>
          </task>
        </exercise>
        <exercise>
          <task>
            <statement>
              <p>Use the <c>statsmodels</c> package to fit the model <md>\texttt{Sales}=\beta_0+\beta_1\,\texttt{TV}+\beta_2\,\texttt{Radio}+\beta_3\,\texttt{Newspaper}.</md> What is the equation for the fitted model?</p>
              <program language="python">
                <code>
                  # Fit Sales using TV, Radio, and Newspaper.
                  est = smf.ols(..., data=advertising_df).fit()
                  est.params
                </code>
              </program>
            </statement>
            <hint>
              <p>Use the formula string <c>'Sales ~ TV + Radio + Newspaper'</c> in <c>smf.ols</c>.</p>
            </hint>
            <hint>
              <p>Read the intercept and slopes from <c>est.params</c>, keeping each coefficient matched to its predictor.</p>
            </hint>
            <hint>
              <p><alert>Common error:</alert> Keep the small negative sign on the <c>Newspaper</c> coefficient; rounding it to <c>-0.00</c> can hide its direction.</p>
            </hint>
            <answer>
              <p><m>\widehat{Sales}=2.9389+0.045765\,TV+0.188530\,Radio-0.001037\,Newspaper</m>.</p>
              <program language="python">
                <code>
                  est = smf.ols('Sales ~ TV + Radio + Newspaper', data=advertising_df).fit()
                  est.params
                </code>
              </program>
            </answer>
          </task>
        </exercise>
        <exercise>
          <task>
            <statement>
              <p>Use the <c>summary</c> command for the trained model class to determine the F-statistic for this model.</p>
              <program language="python">
                <code>
                  # Display the fitted model summary.
                  ...
                </code>
              </program>
            </statement>
            <hint>
              <p>Call <c>est.summary()</c> on the fitted model from the previous exercise.</p>
            </hint>
            <hint>
              <p>Find both <c>F-statistic</c> and <c>Prob (F-statistic)</c> near the top of the summary.</p>
            </hint>
            <hint>
              <p><alert>Common error:</alert> Do not report <m>R^2=0.897</m> as the F-statistic; they are different summary quantities.</p>
            </hint>
            <answer>
              <p>The global F-statistic is approximately <m>570.3</m>, with p-value <m>1.58\times10^{-96}</m>.</p>
              <program language="python">
                <code>
                  est.summary()
                </code>
              </program>
            </answer>
          </task>
        </exercise>
        <exercise>
          <task>
            <statement>
              <p>What are the null and alternative hypotheses for the test this statistic is used for?</p>
            </statement>
            <hint>
              <p>The global F-test asks whether the predictor slopes are jointly zero.</p>
            </hint>
            <hint>
              <p>The alternative only needs one slope to be nonzero; it does not require every predictor to be useful.</p>
            </hint>
            <hint>
              <p><alert>Common error:</alert> The null concerns the three slope coefficients, not the intercept, and the alternative is not that all three slopes are nonzero.</p>
            </hint>
            <answer>
              <p><m>H_0:\beta_{TV}=\beta_{Radio}=\beta_{Newspaper}=0</m>. The alternative is <m>H_A:</m> at least one of these three slope coefficients is nonzero.</p>
            </answer>
          </task>
        </exercise>
        <exercise>
          <task>
            <statement>
              <p>What is your conclusion of the test given this F-score?</p>
            </statement>
            <hint>
              <p>Compare <c>Prob (F-statistic)</c> with a conventional significance level such as <m>\alpha=0.05</m>.</p>
            </hint>
            <hint>
              <p>State the decision about <m>H_0</m> and then translate it into a conclusion about the predictors as a group.</p>
            </hint>
            <hint>
              <p><alert>Common error:</alert> The tiny p-value is not the probability that the null hypothesis is true, and the global F-test does not identify which predictor is associated with <c>Sales</c>.</p>
            </hint>
            <answer>
              <p>Because <m>p=1.58\times10^{-96}&lt;0.05</m>, reject <m>H_0</m>. The data provide very strong evidence that at least one of <c>TV</c>, <c>Radio</c>, and <c>Newspaper</c> has a nonzero linear association with <c>Sales</c>. The global test alone does not say which one.</p>
            </answer>
          </task>
        </exercise>
        <exercise>
          <task>
            <statement>
              <p>List all <alert>7 nonempty subsets</alert> of the three predictors <c>TV</c>, <c>Radio</c>, and <c>Newspaper</c>.</p>
            </statement>
            <hint>
              <p>A set with three elements has <m>2^3=8</m> subsets when the empty set is included.</p>
            </hint>
            <hint>
              <p>Organize the nonempty subsets by size: three one-variable subsets, three two-variable subsets, and one three-variable subset.</p>
            </hint>
            <hint>
              <p><alert>Common error:</alert> The original notebook said six, but there are seven nonempty subsets; omitting the full three-variable subset is a common counting error.</p>
            </hint>
            <answer>
              <p>The seven nonempty subsets are: <c>{TV}</c>, <c>{Radio}</c>, <c>{Newspaper}</c>, <c>{TV, Radio}</c>, <c>{TV, Newspaper}</c>, <c>{Radio, Newspaper}</c>, and <c>{TV, Radio, Newspaper}</c>.</p>
            </answer>
          </task>
        </exercise>
        <exercise>
          <task>
            <statement>
              <p>Below is a command to get the RSS for the statsmodel linear fit. For each of the subsets listed above, what is the RSS for the learned model? Which is smallest?</p>
              <program language="python">
                <code>
                  import itertools

                  def statsmodelRSS(est):
                      return np.sum(est.resid**2)

                  variables = ['TV', 'Radio', 'Newspaper']
                  # Loop over subset sizes 1, 2, and 3; fit each model and print its RSS.
                  ...
                </code>
              </program>
            </statement>
            <hint>
              <p>Use <c>itertools.combinations(variables, size)</c> for subset sizes 1, 2, and 3, and build each formula with <c>' + '.join(subset)</c>.</p>
            </hint>
            <hint>
              <p>For every fitted model, record <c>est.ssr</c> (or <c>statsmodelRSS(est)</c>) and track the smallest value.</p>
            </hint>
            <hint>
              <p><alert>Common error:</alert> Training RSS cannot increase when another predictor is added, so the full model will have the smallest training RSS; this alone does not prove it will predict new data best.</p>
            </hint>
            <answer>
              <p>The RSS values are: <c>TV</c> <m>2102.53</m>; <c>Radio</c> <m>3618.48</m>; <c>Newspaper</c> <m>5134.80</m>; <c>TV + Radio</c> <m>556.91</m>; <c>TV + Newspaper</c> <m>1918.56</m>; <c>Radio + Newspaper</c> <m>3614.84</m>; and <c>TV + Radio + Newspaper</c> <m>556.83</m>. The full three-predictor model has the smallest training RSS, although it is only slightly below the <c>TV + Radio</c> model.</p>
              <program language="python">
                <code>
                  import itertools

                  variables = ['TV', 'Radio', 'Newspaper']
                  results = []
                  for size in range(1, len(variables) + 1):
                      for subset in itertools.combinations(variables, size):
                          formula = 'Sales ~ ' + ' + '.join(subset)
                          fitted = smf.ols(formula, data=advertising_df).fit()
                          results.append((subset, fitted.ssr))

                  for subset, rss in results:
                      print(subset, round(rss, 2))

                  print('Smallest:', min(results, key=lambda item: item[1]))
                </code>
              </program>
            </answer>
          </task>
        </exercise>
      </exercisegroup>
    </exercises>
